{"book":"Job","chapter":4,"evidence_availability":"AVAILABLE","failure_reason":null,"generated_metadata":{"commentary_prompt_version":"1.8","commentary_schema_version":"1.2","evidence_bundle_version":"1.1","evidence_hash":"6991aedc721ab854a491a81b5369161478b38e216c08941933b67aca1d1062ce","generated_timestamp":null,"model":"gpt-5.6-terra","renderer_label":"commentary-v1.2-codex-session","synthesis_compiler_version":"1.1","synthesis_hash":"40bf4f336158d48f84316d5260d56d755d66d70ce8aced2a5b02e12d99e576f7","synthesis_schema_version":"1.1"},"reference":"Job 4","sections":[{"blocks":[{"confidence":"high","evidence_ids":["job-lament-dialogues","job:interpretive_note:11","job:interpretive_note:4"],"id":"block_1","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_surrounding_passages_04d5cd1fe568"],"text":"Job 4 begins the sustained debate that follows Job’s lament over the day of his birth. Eliphaz’s opening speech praises Job for strengthening others, then argues that the innocent do not perish and that those who sow wrongdoing reap trouble. Across the dialogue, Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar increasingly interpret Job’s suffering as evidence of guilt, while Job rejects that diagnosis. Their earlier seven days of silent presence stand in sharp contrast to counsel that later minimizes Job’s testimony and treats suffering as proof; the book’s ending rebukes the friends, so their speeches should not be received as undifferentiated advice for someone who suffers.","verse_refs":["Job 10:1-22","Job 11:1-20","Job 12:1-25","Job 13:1-28","Job 14:1-22","Job 15:1-35","Job 16:1-22","Job 17:1-16","Job 18:1-21","Job 19:1-29","Job 1:1-22","Job 20:1-29","Job 21:1-34","Job 22:1-30","Job 23:1-17","Job 24:1-25","Job 25:1-6","Job 26:1-14","Job 27:1-23","Job 28:1-28","Job 29:1-25","Job 2:1-13","Job 2:11-13","Job 30:1-31","Job 31:1-40","Job 32:1-22","Job 33:1-33","Job 34:1-37","Job 35:1-16","Job 36:1-33","Job 37:1-24","Job 38:1-41","Job 39:1-30","Job 3:1-26","Job 40:1-24","Job 41:1-34","Job 4